## Lifetime value and the allowance

BG/NBD (how often a customer buys, and whether they are still a customer) and Gamma-Gamma (how
much they spend when they buy) were fit on the {{ v('clv.customers') }} Online Retail II customers
who bought by {{ v('clv.calibration_end') }}, of {{ v('clv.customers_in_file') }} in the file, and
judged on what those customers did from {{ v('clv.holdout_start') }} to {{ v('clv.holdout_end') }}.

{% call m.holds(raw('clv.holdout.purchases_predicted_over_actual') > 0 and raw('clv.holdout.top_decile_purchases_predicted_over_actual') < raw('clv.holdout.purchases_predicted_over_actual'), 'that BG/NBD over predicts the holdout and the top decile is closer') %}
**The model over predicts.** It expected {{ v('clv.holdout.predicted_purchases_total') }} purchases in
the holdout and the customers made {{ v('clv.holdout.actual_purchases_total') }}, a difference of
{{ v('clv.holdout.purchases_predicted_over_actual') }}; revenue is over by
{{ v('clv.holdout.revenue_predicted_over_actual') }}. The cause is in the parameters: the fitted chance
that a customer stops buying after a purchase is {{ v('clv.bgnbd.dropout_after_purchase') }}, close to
zero, so the model treats nearly every customer who went quiet as still active and keeps predicting
purchases for them. Among the customers who bought most often in the calibration window, the tenth
decile, the gap is much smaller: {{ v('clv.holdout.top_decile_purchases_predicted_over_actual') }}.
{% endcall %}

{{ table('clv.holdout_by_decile') }}

{{ label('clv.holdout_by_decile') }} Deciles are of purchase frequency in the calibration window.

PyMC-Marketing fit the same two models to the same frame, by maximum a posteriori under flat
priors; the largest relative difference across the seven parameters is
{{ v('clv.crosscheck.max_relative_difference') }}. The two fits agree, which points the over prediction
at the model on this data rather than at the repository's code.

{{ table('clv.parameters') }}

